"A327094 - OEIS (https://oeis.org/A327094/internal)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.1 years ago; Crawled: 7 months ago; a(n) is the number of cells in the smallest polyomino that can contain all free n-ominoes. ... `%C From _Peter Exley_, Mar 08 2026: (Start) `\n\n(Greetings from The On-Line Encyclopedia of Integer Sequences!)\n\nA327094\n\na(n) is the number of cells in the smallest polyomino that can contain all free n-ominoes.\n\n`%I #35 Mar 27 2026 00:14:41 `\n\n`%S 0,1,2,4,6,9,12,17,20,26,31 `\n\n`%N a(n) is the number of cells in the smallest polyomino that can contain all free n-ominoes. `\n\n`%C a(n) <= n*(n - 1)/2 for n > 1, by using a right triangular polyomino with the topmost cell moved to the bottom row. `\n\n`%C Conjecture: a(9) = 26, a(10) = 31, a(11) = 37, and a(12) = 43. `\n\n`%C From _Peter Exley_, Mar 08 2026: (Start) `\n\n`%C a(9) = 26 confirmed by exact SAT solver using PySAT/CaDiCaL. The solver uses Boolean variables for cell occupancy on an n X n grid, auxiliary variables for each valid placement of each free n-omino, a cardinality constraint for the cell count, and iterative connectivity cuts. Shape constraints (contiguous rows, exactly one full row of width n, symmetry breaking) reduce the search space. Top-down search from upper bound proves optimality: SAT at k=26 (solution exists), UNSAT at k=25 (no solution with 25 cells). All previously known terms a(0)-a(8) matched by solver. `\n\n`%C The upper bound a(n) <= n*(n-1)/2 holds for n >= 4 but not for n = 2 or n = 3. (End) `\n\n`%C From _Peter Exley_, Mar 23 2026: (Start) `\n\n`%C a(10) = 31, proved by CP-SAT (OR-Tools) with CEGAR connectivity. SAT at k=31 on 5 X 10 grid (54.9s). UNSAT at k=30 proved on all grids satisfying the spanning bound (H+W-1 <= 30, H >= 5, W >= 10), with I-piece symmetry break. Cross-validated using CaDiCaL. `\n\n`%C The optimal container for n=10 has the \"staircase\" structure with row widths 10, 8, 6, 4, 3 on a 5 X 10 grid. `\n\n`%C Conjecture: a(n) = floor((n+1)^2/4) + 1 for n >= 9. This matches the conjectured values a(11) = 37, a(12) = 43. The formula floor((n+1)^2/4) = A002620(n+1) counts cells in the \"staircase\" polyomino with row widths n, n-2, n-4, ..., which is optimal for n <= 6 and n = 8 but requires one extra cell for n >= 9. `\n\n`%C Observation: a(n) >= floor((n+1)^2/4) for all n, with equality for n = 0..6 and n = 8. The staircase lower bound arises because the container must span at least ceil(n/2) rows (spanning bound theorem: |P| >= H + W - 1 for connected polyomino P) and n columns (to contain the straight n-omino). `\n\n`%C All optimal containers for n = 3..10 have bounding box ceiling(n/2) X n. (End) `\n\n`%H Code Golf Stack Exchange, <a href=\"https://codegolf.stackexchange.com/q/167484/53884\">Smallest region of the plane that contains all free n-ominoes</a> `\n\n`%H Peter Exley, <a href=\"https://github.com/pexley-math/oeis-A327094\">Solver code, data, and figures</a>, GitHub. `\n\n`%e For n = 5 the smallest polyomino that contains all 5-ominos is a polyomino with a(5) = 9 cells. One such 9-omino that works is `\n\n`%e ### `\n\n`%e #####. `\n\n`%e # `\n\n`%e For example, the \"L\"-shaped, \"+\"-shaped, and \"I\"-shaped 5-ominoes fit in the following ways: `\n\n`%e All other 5-ominoes can fit into this 9-omino too. `\n\n`%e From _Peter Exley_, Mar 08 2026: (Start) `\n\n`%e For n = 9, the minimal container has a(9) = 26 cells: `\n\n`%e . (End) `\n\n`%e From _Peter Exley_, Mar 23 2026: (Start) `\n\n`%e For n = 10, the smallest container has a(10) = 31 cells on a 5 X 10 grid: `\n\n`%e (End) `\n\n`%Y Cf. A000105, A002620 (quarter-squares: staircase lower bound). `\n\n`%K nonn,more `\n\n`%O 0,3 `\n\n`%A _Peter Kagey_, Sep 13 2019 `\n\n`%E a(9) from _Peter Exley_, Mar 08 2026 `\n\n`%E a(10) from _Peter Exley_, Mar 23 2026 `--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - pexley-math/oeis-A352029: Paper and figures for OEIS A352029 -- a(n) is the number of minimalist polyominoes that can contain all n-ominoes. · GitHub (https://github.com/pexley-math/oeis-A352029)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Peter Exley, Independent Researcher, Brisbane, Australia. ... A connected polyomino `C` such that every free n-omino can be placed inside `C` by some rotation, reflection, and translation.\n\nName  | Name  | Last commit message  | Last commit date\n--- | --- | --- | ---\nLICENSE  | LICENSE  |    |  \nREADME.md  | README.md  |    |  \nfigures.pdf  | figures.pdf  |    |  \n\n## Repository files navigation\n\n  *   * README\n  * License\n\nMore items\n\n# Counting the minimalist polyomino containers\n\nPeter Exley, Independent Researcher, Brisbane, Australia. Submitted: Jun 03 2026.\n\nFix a number of cells `n` and write down every distinct shape we can build from `n` unit squares joined edge to edge. We ask for a single shape that contains all of them at once -- each piece allowed to rotate, reflect, and slide into place -- using as few cells as possible. Several different shapes can tie for that smallest size. How many are there? That count is the subject of this paper.\n\n## The problem\n\nA free n-omino is a connected shape made of `n` unit squares, with two shapes considered the same when one can be turned into the other by rotation, reflection, and translation. \nThe sequence is not monotone -- the drop from `204` to `7` rules out any increasing law -- and we found no low-degree polynomial, short constant-coefficient linear recurrence, or closed form in the density that fits the eight terms (a degree-`7` interpolant through eight points always exists but predicts nothing). We searched the OEIS for the sequence, its first differences, and its partial sums; no other match appeared. The density picture is a heuristic for the shape of the sequence only, not a formula; we state it as such (UNVERIFIED) and leave an exact description open.\n\n`a(9)` remains open. At least `111` distinct minimum containers exist there, so `a(9) >= 111`, but a complete enumeration is infeasible: the near-square bounding boxes at `n = 9` are far harder than anything at `n = 8`, and the search does not finish within a practical budget. Proving `a(9)` -- and finding any closed description of where the count rises and falls -- are the open problems.\n\n## Further reading\n\nThis work was inspired by the OEIS and the community of contributors who maintain it.\n\n  * Kagey, P. (2019). A327094: Number of cells in the smallest polyomino containing all free n-ominoes. https://oeis.org/A327094\n  * Sloane, N. J. A. A000105: Number of free polyominoes (or square animals) with n cells. https://oeis.org/A000105\n\n## License\n\nCC-BY-4.0 -- see `LICENSE`. This work is freely available; a citation or acknowledgment is appreciated but not required.\n\n## About\n\nPaper and figures for OEIS A352029 -- a(n) is the number of minimalist polyominoes that can contain all n-ominoes.\n\nReadme\n\nLicense\n\nActivity\n\nCustom properties\n\n### Stars\n\n1 star\n\n### Watchers\n\n0 watching\n\n### Forks\n\n0 forks--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA396884 - OEIS (https://oeis.org/A396884)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; Peter Exley, Paper and figures, GitHub. ... A000577 (free n-iamonds, the shapes to be contained), A001420 (fixed n-iamonds), A352029 (square-lattice analog, minimalist polyomino containers), A392363 (minimum container size for size n).\n\nA396884\n\nNumber of distinct smallest connected polyiamonds that contain every free n-iamond, up to rotations, reflections and translations of the triangular lattice.\n\n1\n\n1, 1, 1, 1, 4, 5, 39, 13, 26\n\nOFFSET\n\n1,5\n\nCOMMENTS\n\nA polyiamond is an edge-connected set of unit triangles drawn from the regular triangular tiling of the plane (vertex configuration 3.3.3.3.3.3, symmetry group p6m), whose cells alternate between upward- and downward-pointing triangles. A free n-iamond is one of the A000577(n) shapes of n such triangles, counted up to rotation and reflection.\n\nA container for size n is a connected polyiamond that holds a copy of every free n-iamond as a sub-shape under the lattice symmetries; a(n) counts the containers of the smallest such size, themselves counted up to rotation, reflection and translation. That smallest size is A392363(n).\n\nThe sequence is non-monotonic: a(7) = 39, a(8) = 13, a(9) = 26. The square-lattice analog A352029 swings the same way, peaking at 204 then dropping to 7, so a jump in the minimum size A392363(n) can sharply cut the number of distinct minimal containers.\n\nNo closed form or low-order recurrence is known for the nine listed terms (conjectured to have none, UNVERIFIED). The listed values are the number of minimum-size containers found within a finite search window whose width equals n (forced by the straight n-iamond) and whose height grows as max(4, floor((n+2)/3)); global minimality over all bounding boxes is conjectured but not separately certified.\n\nLINKS\n\nTable of n, a(n) for n=1..9.\n\nPeter Exley, Paper and figures, GitHub.\n\nCROSSREFS\n\nCf. A000577 (free n-iamonds, the shapes to be contained), A001420 (fixed n-iamonds), A352029 (square-lattice analog, minimalist polyomino containers), A392363 (minimum container size for size n).\n\nKEYWORD\n\nnonn,more,hard\n\nAUTHOR\n\nPeter Exley, Jun 09 2026\n\nSTATUS\n\napproved--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPeter EXLEY personal appointments - Find and update company information - GOV.UK (https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/officers/D6jJmYJDrYWIZNKqJqO6lQ4LZTs/appointments)\nciteturn1search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; # Peter EXLEY\n\n# Peter EXLEY\n\n## Filter appointments\n\nFilter appointments, selecting an input will reload the page. [Input] Current appointments [Input]\n\n## Total number of appointments 1\n\nDate of birth\n    September 1988\n\n## CROWN LANE GARDENS (STREATHAM) RESIDENTS ASSOCIATION LIMITED (00892730)\n\nCompany status\n    Active\n\nCorrespondence address\n    Stonemead House, 95 London Road, Croydon, Surrey, United Kingdom, CR0 2RF\n\nRole Resigned\n    Director\n\nAppointed on\n    23 January 2025\n\nResigned on\n    20 April 2026\n\nNationality\n    British\n\nPlace of residence\n    United Kingdom\n\nIdentity verification due\n    7 May 2026\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - pexley-math/oeis-A369367: Paper and figures for OEIS A369367 -- number of free n-celled polyiamonds with the least number (a369366(n)) of inequivalent cells. · GitHub (https://github.com/pexley-math/oeis-A369367)\nciteturn1search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # Counting the most symmetric polyiamonds: OEIS A369367\n\nPeter Exley, Independent Researcher, Brisbane, Australia.\n\n# Counting the most symmetric polyiamonds: OEIS A369367\n\nPeter Exley, Independent Researcher, Brisbane, Australia. Submitted: Jun 02 2026.\n\nA polyiamond is a shape made of unit triangles joined edge to edge. Colour two of its cells the same when a symmetry of the shape carries one to the other; the number of resulting colours is the shape's count of inequivalent cells. Among all n-triangle shapes, some achieve the fewest possible inequivalent cells -- they are, in this precise sense, the most symmetric. This paper counts them. The count is startlingly erratic: one shape at n = 16, then 523 at n = 17.\n\n## The problem\n\nFix the number of cells n. Over all free n-iamonds, A369366(n) is the least number of inequivalent cells any of them has. A369367(n), the sequence of this paper, counts how many free n-iamonds achieve that minimum. This is OEIS A369367.\n\n\"Inequivalent cells\" is made precise through symmetry. Every polyiamond has an automorphism group: the rigid motions of the triangular grid that map the shape onto itself. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nACE Cultural Tours (https://www.aceculturaltours.co.uk/component/tags/tags/peter-exley)\nciteturn1search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; # Peter Exley\n\n# Peter Exley\n\n[Input] Show Departed Tours\n\nWild & Ancient Sardinia April 7–14, 2026\n\nWildlife & Walking on the Isles of Scilly May 12–19, 2026\n\nThe Shetland Islands June 22–29, 2026\n\nWild & Ancient Orkney July 7–14, 2026\n\nWild & Ancient Anglesey September 14–19, 2026\n\nSomerset: History, Legend & Natural Wonders February 10–13, 2027 Tour Director: Peter Exley\n\nSpring on Dartmoor May, 2027 Tour Director: Peter Exley\n\nOrkney: Natural & Neolithic Wonders July 8–15, 2027 Tour Director: Peter Exley\n\nDorset: Landscapes & Seascapes October, 2027 Tour Director: Peter Exley\n\nGet in Touch\n\n### Get in Touch\n\nOur sales team are available to help Monday - Friday 9am - 5pm. You can contact us by phone or email or by completing an enquiry form below.\nTo receive our latest tour details and news, request our most recent brochure or join our email newsletter, use the buttons below.\n+44(0)1223 841055\nsales@aceculturaltours.co.uk\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMathematical_Magic_Show_More_Puzzles,_Games,_Diversions,_Illusions - Flipbook by PSS MEGAH BESTARI SMK ALAM MEGAH 2 | FlipHTML5 (https://fliphtml5.com/lrdpq/nydx/Mathematical_Magic_Show_More_Puzzles%2C_Games%2C_Diversions%2C_Illusions/)\nciteturn1search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.7 years ago; Crawled: 2 days ago; Polyominoes and Rectification ADDENDUM GOLOMB'PSENTOMINO game was first described in my Scien- tific American column, November 1957 (reprinted in the first collection of columns).\nP:185\n\nPolyominoes and Rectification ADDENDUM GOLOMB'PSENTOMINO game was first described in my Scien- tific American column, November 1957 (reprinted in the first collection of columns). Since then, two unauthorized versions of the game have been marketed. The first, called Pan-K5i (Phillips Publishers, Newton, Mass., 1960), used an order-10 board and a set of 12 pentominoes for each of two players. In 1967, Parker Brothers brought out Universe. The field was shaped like a fat cross, and four sets of pentominoes were in- cluded so that two, three, or four players could compete. As in Pan-KCi, a rule prevents placing any piece so as to create an enclosed area with fewer than five cells. The box's cover dis- played a scene from the motion picture 2001: A Space Odyssey showing the game played on a computer aboard the spaceship. When the movie was later released, however, this episode had been replaced by a computer chess game. The first authorized version of the game, with an instruction booklet prepared by Golomb, was marketed in 1973 by the Springbok Division of Hallmark Cards, just twenty years after Golomb introduced polyominoes to mathematicians in his mem- orable talk to the Harvard Mathematics Club. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nReceational Mathematics | PDF | Recreational Mathematics | Popular Scholarship (https://www.scribd.com/document/399956215/Receational-Mathematics)\nciteturn1search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 14.8 years ago; Crawled: 2.6 years ago; Free, one-sided, and fixed polyominoes ... 11 undecomino 17,073 979 16,094 33,896 135,268\n(Think of pieces that can be\npicked up and flipped over.)\n one-sided polyominoes are distinct when none is a translation or rotation of\nanother. (Think of pieces that cannot be flipped over.)\n fixed polyominoes are distinct when none is a translation of another. (Think of\npieces that can be neither flipped nor rotated.)\n\nThe following table shows the numbers of polyominoes of various types with n cells. Let\nAn denote the number of fixed polyominoes with n cells (possibly with holes).\n\nfree free\nfree with\n(sequence without one-sided fixed\nn name holes\nA000105 in holes (A000988) (An, A001168)\n(A001419)\nOEIS) (A000104)\n1 monomino 1 0 1 1 1\n2 domino 1 0 1 1 2\ntromino or\n3 2 0 2 2 6\ntriomino\n4 tetromino 5 0 5 7 19\n5 pentomino 12 0 12 18 63\n6 hexomino 35 0 35 60 216\n7 heptomino 108 1 107 196 760\n8 octomino 369 6 363 704 2,725\nnonomino or\n9 1,285 37 1,248 2,500 9,910\nenneomino\n10 decomino 4,655 195 4,460 9,189 36,446\n11 undecomino 17,073 979 16,094 33,896 135,268\n12 dodecomino 63,600 4,663 58,937 126,759 505,861\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRob's Puzzle Page - Assembly and Packing (https://www.robspuzzlepage.com/assembly.htm)\nciteturn1search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2.6 years ago; Sometimes the puzzle is presented with a subset of all the pieces except for one of them packed into the container, with seemingly no room for the additional piece, and the objective being to rearrange the pieces to make the last piece fit, too. ... (For those of a mathematical bent, free polyominoes are equivalence classes of fixed polyominoes under dihedral group D4.) ... A gift from Brett of three \"Meiji Chocolate\" plastic Polyomino puzzles by Hanayama - Milk (12 pentominoes), Black (11 hexominoes), and White (8 pieces) - find them at Kinokuniya.\nAgain, useful for computer analysis but usually all rotations are considered identical when enumerating physical pieces.\n  * reflection - flipping a 2D piece over to obtain its mirror image, or reflecting a 3D piece. This operation is sometimes legal, sometimes not, depending on the puzzle. Sometimes it is useful to know the total number of pieces including mirror images.\n\n* * *\n\nWhen enumerating piece sets, it is important to know how to treat each of the operations. There are usually three figures of interest:\n\n  * free pieces - this is the number of distinct pieces excluding translations, rotations, and reflections. In other words, a given shape is free to move, turn, and flip in any way, so all such orientations count as the same piece.\n  * one-sided pieces - excludes translations and rotations but includes reflections. This is the count of interest if flipping a piece is not allowed, or if you're dealing with solid polyforms whose mirror images are considered distinct.\n  * fixed pieces - excludes translations but includes both rotations and reflections. (For those of a mathematical bent, free polyominoes are equivalence classes of fixed polyominoes under dihedral group D4.)\n\n* * *\n\nBelow is a chart of the number of pieces as n grows. Also see Michael Keller's page, Polyomino Enumerations, Joseph Myers' page, and Miroslav Vicher's page. There is no formula known which will give the exact number of all possible pieces given a number of unit elements.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFour-arm polyominoes: proof appendix — Angel Ivanov Raychev (https://angelraychev.com/polyominoes/proofs/)\nciteturn1search9 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: last month; # Four-arm polyominoes: proof appendix ... The universal unequal half-plane obstruction a>b>=3,a>=5 now compiles as `PolyominoFormal.LHalfPlaneUnequal.no_halfplane`.\n\n# Four-arm polyominoes: proof appendix\n\n11 Sep 2026\n\nThis appendix records the mathematical arguments and precise certificate boundaries. The companion article provides the classification table and illustrations; the research log preserves conjectures, unsuccessful approaches, and independent attacks.\n\nThe standalone paper (PDF) organizes the complete classification for publication. Its paper page provides LaTeX sources, citation, verified references, research history, and acknowledgments.\n\nWe identify cells with integer coordinates and use\n\n$$P(a,b,c,d)=\\{(x,0):-c\\le x\\le a\\}\\cup\\{(0,y):-d\\le y\\le b\\}.$$P(a,b,c,d)={(x,0):−c≤x≤a}∪{(0,y):−d≤y≤b}.\n\nAll parameters are nonnegative integers. Every placement is an integer translate of a D4 rotation or reflection. Exact tiling means every region cell is covered exactly once and no tile has a cell outside that region. Here N={0,1,2,…}. A rep-tiling uses congruent original tiles to tile an integer enlargement at a natural linear scale at least two.\nThe universal n>=6 replay contains 8,309 arithmetic lemmas and five full geometric transition proofs. There is no upper bound on n.\n\n### Complete classification\n\nThe unconditional entry point is `PolyominoFormal.all_tuples_classified` in MainClassification.lean:\n    \n    `theorem all_tuples_classified (a b c d : Nat) :\n        Classified a b c d (classifyTuple a b c d)`\n\nIt proves all eight capabilities: rectangle, half-strip, bent strip, quadrant, strip, half-plane, plane, and integer-scale rep-tiling. Natural arms include zero, so bars and degenerate tuples are covered. The theorem has no remaining geometric or obstruction premise. `TupleNormalization` proves the exhaustive reduction and the required D4 equivalences.\n\nFormal result  | Scope\n--- | ---\n`MainClassification`: `all_tuples_classified`  | All four-tuples and all eight capabilities\n`MainClassification`: `sorted_L_classified`  | Every sorted L shape and its degenerate bars\n`TClassification`: `normalized_T_classified`  | Every genuine T\n`PositiveCrossClassification`: `classify_positive_cross`  | Every four-positive-arm cross\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPeter Exley (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_Exley)\nciteturn1search12 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Peter Exley\nPeter Exley\nAmerican architect (born 1964)\nPeter Exley\n\nPeter Exley in 2013\n\nBorn | August 2, 1964 (age 62)\nEducation | Newcastle University (B.A. Hons.,1985), University of Pennsylvania (M.Arch.,1990)\nOccupation | Architect\n\nPractice | Architecture Is Fun, Inc.\nPeter Exley (born August 2, 1964 in Harrogate, England) is the co-founder of Architecture Is Fun, a Chicago-based architecture and design firm. Exley's projects include the DuPage Children's Museum, the House in the Woods, a 1,950 m^{2} (21,000 sq ft) Ronald McDonald House in Oak Lawn, Illinois, the Exploration Station children's museum and the Young at Art Museum's exhibits and galleries in Davie, Florida.\n## Biography\n\nExley earned a Bachelor of Arts in Architectural Studies with first-class honours from Newcastle University School of Architecture, Planning and Landscape in 1985, and a Master of Architecture from the University of Pennsylvania in 1990. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPeter Exley - Architecture Is Fun, Inc. | LinkedIn (https://www.linkedin.com/in/peter-exley-25b2605)\nciteturn1search10 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.1 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # Peter Exley ... Peter Exley’s career is committed to an equitable and inclusive built environment for…\n\n# Peter Exley\n\n### \n\nChicago, Illinois, United States\n\n7K followers 500+ connections\n\n\nWebsites\n\n## Websites\n\nCompany Website\n    http://www.architectureisfun.com\n\n\n## About\n\nPeter Exley’s career is committed to an equitable and inclusive built environment for…\n\n## Services\n\n  * Architecture\n  * Team Building\n  * Public Speaking\n  * Change Management\n  * Strategic Planning\n  * Management Consulting\n\n## Experience\n\n  * ### \n\n#### Architecture Is Fun, Inc.\n\nChicago\n\n  * ### \n\n#### -\n\nAberdeen, Scotland, United Kingdom\n\n  * ### \n\n#### -\n\n37 S Wabash, Chicago, IL 60603\n\n## Education\n\n  * ### University of Pennsylvania\n\n#### -\n\n1988 - 1990\n\nActivities and Societies: Penn Club of Chicago\n\n  * ### \n\n#### -\n\n1982 - 1985\n\n  * ### \n\n#### -\n\n2002 - 2010\n\n## Licenses & Certifications\n\n  * ### Architect\n\n#### New York State Education Department\n\nIssued Jun 2015\n\nCredential ID 037975-1\n\n  * ### Architect\n\n#### State of Wisconsin\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPeter Exley | MAS Context (https://mascontext.com/contributors/peter-exley)\nciteturn1search11 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; # Peter Exley\n\n# Peter Exley\n\nAbout\n\nPeter Exley’s career is committed to an equitable and inclusive built environment for children, families, and communities. As co-founder of Architecture Is Fun, he uses the power of design to build places and spaces that reflect the values, ambitions, and purpose of every stakeholder on myriad civic, cultural, private, and not-for-profit agencies. As a Dean and teacher, he is nurturing future architects as the primary and credible resource with the explicit training and qualification to lead the creation of healthy and equitable net-zero place-making. As the 97th President of the American Institute of Architects, he led AIA’s delegation to COP26 in Glasgow, and began the AIA Headquarters renewal project embodying the urgent need for climate action alongside a dramatic transformation for workplace, member, and community experience.\n\nLinks\n\n  * architectureisfun.com\n  * @funarchitect\n\nArticle\n\nNortherly Island at 100\n\nCollective reflection on Northerly Island as it turns 100\n\nRemembrance\n\nHelmut Jahn (1940-2021)\n\nRemembering architect Helmut Jahn and his work\n\nEvent\n\nTracing / Traces: Architecture and the Archive 2020--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAIA Convention 2020: (https://content.aia.org/sites/default/files/2020-07/2020_Official_Delegate_Information_Booklet.pdf)\nciteturn1search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Peter Exley, FAIA, First Vice President\n2020 Official Delegate Information Booklet \n \n2020 Board of Directors \nL. Jane Frederick, FAIA, President \nPeter Exley, FAIA, First Vice President \nJason C. Winters, AIA, Secretary \nEvelyn Lee, AIA, Treasurer \nGeorge B. “Barney” Forsythe, PhD, Director  \nEmily Grandstaff-Rice, FAIA, At-large Director \nDaniel Hart, FAIA, At-large Director \nTimothy Hawk, FAI